WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.925
Atomic writes make a batch all-or-nothing.

2
00:00:02.925 --> 00:00:07.543
This episode follows the single decision
that lets readers see it as one change.

3
00:00:08.543 --> 00:00:11.010
A batch can update several keys
in one tree,

4
00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.758
such as an order's status
and its tracking value.

5
00:00:14.108 --> 00:00:15.823
A coordinator groups the keys

6
00:00:15.823 --> 00:00:19.727
by their owning shards and stages
each change as a prepared write.

7
00:00:20.077 --> 00:00:25.057
Each leaf keeps its prepared values
in a pending bucket for this transaction.

8
00:00:25.057 --> 00:00:27.838
They are not visible
to concurrent readers.

9
00:00:28.188 --> 00:00:29.793
If every prepare succeeds,

10
00:00:29.793 --> 00:00:33.435
the saga records commit
in the tree's transaction registry.

11
00:00:33.785 --> 00:00:37.047
That registry write is
the tree-wide visibility flip.

12
00:00:37.397 --> 00:00:40.664
A read that finds a pending key
resolves it through the registry's

13
00:00:40.664 --> 00:00:44.031
recorded outcome, rather than guessing
from which leaf has finished.

14
00:00:44.381 --> 00:00:47.075
A multi-key reader uses
one decision snapshot.

15
00:00:47.075 --> 00:00:49.769
It sees the batch's full effect
or none of it,

16
00:00:49.769 --> 00:00:52.229
never one prepared key without the others.

17
00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:53.637
After the decision,

18
00:00:53.637 --> 00:00:57.870
terminal marks travel to the touched
leaves and drain their pending buckets.

19
00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:01.212
On the originating tree,
a read of a key still in a prepared

20
00:01:01.212 --> 00:01:05.333
bucket can follow the recorded outcome
while terminal marks are in flight.

21
00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:09.677
The batch's logical visibility does
not wait for every leaf to apply its mark.

22
00:01:10.027 --> 00:01:12.417
The leaves finish applying the outcome;

23
00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:15.236
they do not make a second
visibility decision.

24
00:01:16.236 --> 00:01:18.690
If a prepare fails,
the coordinator retries.

25
00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:22.426
If the batch still cannot complete,
the saga records abort instead.

26
00:01:22.776 --> 00:01:27.250
The prepared values never became visible,
so abort drops their buckets.

27
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.338
It does not write a rollback value
for every key.

28
00:01:30.688 --> 00:01:34.245
That matters when another writer
changed a key during the saga:

29
00:01:34.245 --> 00:01:37.350
rollback writes could overwrite
that concurrent change.

30
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.104
There is coordination:
the saga coordinator drives the work

31
00:01:41.104 --> 00:01:44.219
and learns whether the shard
fan-out staged the batch.

32
00:01:44.569 --> 00:01:47.361
But the leaves do not reach a commit
by consensus.

33
00:01:47.361 --> 00:01:49.204
The registry records one outcome,

34
00:01:49.204 --> 00:01:51.941
and readers use that outcome
for the whole batch.

35
00:01:52.291 --> 00:01:57.225
For the protocol's verification and design
mapping, read Verified atomic commit.

36
00:01:57.575 --> 00:02:00.619
Back on Build, next: TTL and soft delete.

37
00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:04.926
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.
